# Source is text, and git should keep saying so.
#
# git auto-detects binary by sniffing for a NUL byte in the first 8000 bytes. A
# single literal NUL in a source file — easy to write by accident as a join
# separator — flips that detection, and then `git diff` prints
# `Bin 7463 -> 9147 bytes` instead of code, `git blame` has nothing to
# attribute, and `grep`/ripgrep skip the file silently. That happened to
# apps/fleet-executor/src/verdict.ts and cost real debugging time twice.
# THE MERGE FAILURE, AND WHY NO ATTRIBUTE HERE FIXES IT.
# The same NUL sniff is applied by git's three-way merge. When it trips, git
# prints `Cannot merge binary files: <path>`, abandons hunk-level merging, and
# takes one whole side. In 47964247a that is exactly what happened to
# lib/roadmap-items.ts: "ours" was taken wholesale and #9352's ADR-0086
# planner-column work vanished with no conflict marker and a green build. It
# took a hand-run `git merge-file` over de-NUL'd stages (2dcd80f9f) to recover.
# It is tempting to think `merge` below prevents that. It does NOT. The binary
# check lives inside git's built-in merge driver, ahead of the attribute, and
# was measured to be unreachable by configuration: with a raw NUL present,
# `text diff`, `text diff merge`, `merge=union`, and even a custom driver ALL
# still produce `Cannot merge binary files` and still drop a side. The ONLY
# thing that prevents the silent-loss merge is not writing the NUL — which is
# why tests/unit/source-is-text.test.js, not this file, is the real guard.
# WHAT `merge` DOES BUY. It pins the built-in text driver for these extensions
# so a local `merge.default` cannot redirect them. Measured: with
# `merge.default=binary` set in a clone's config and only `text diff` here, an
# ordinary NUL-FREE source file merges as binary and silently loses a side;
# with `merge` set it merges normally. That is a real hole, independent of the
# NUL problem, and this closes it.
# tests/unit/source-is-text.test.js walks every tracked file matching the
# extensions below for raw NULs, and asserts all three attributes stay `set` —
# so "simplifying" this file back into the broken state fails CI.
*.ts   text diff merge
*.tsx  text diff merge
*.js   text diff merge
*.mjs  text diff merge
*.cjs  text diff merge
*.rs   text diff merge
*.json text diff merge
# BINARY MEDIA -> git-lfs, REPO-WIDE.
#
# WHAT THIS REPLACES, AND WHY. Until this change the only LFS rules were three
# path-scoped globs (whitepaper-foundlings/**/*.png|pdf, skill_candidates/**/*.pdf).
# Measured on 0b6d9c21c: those three carried 409 files / 139.8 MiB, while 2,378
# tracked media files held 767.8 MiB as ORDINARY git blobs -- and not one of the
# 25 largest files in HEAD matched any of the three. The configuration pointed
# away from exactly the weight it was added to carry. Repo-wide bare globs make
# the DEFAULT correct, so a new screenshot is a pointer wherever it lands rather
# than only where somebody remembered to add a directory.
#
# SCOPE -- SAY IT PLAINLY. These rules govern FUTURE writes only. A filter does
# not rewrite blobs that are already committed, and this change deliberately
# does NOT re-add the 2,378 existing files. That is not the cheap half of a
# choice, it is very nearly the whole of the available benefit: converting a
# file that is never edited again reclaims nothing (its original blob stays in
# history forever) while adding an LFS object against the storage quota. The
# existing 767.8 MiB is addressed by the R2 offload in #10199 (ADR-0142), and
# by a history rewrite if one is ever scheduled -- not by this file.
*.png   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.jpg   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.jpeg  filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.gif   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.webp  filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.tiff  filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.mp4   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.mov   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.webm  filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.pdf   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

# BUILD INPUTS -- EXEMPT, and the exemption is the safety-critical half.
#
# A file a BUILD reads must never be an LFS pointer. Every consumer that forgets
# `git lfs pull` then gets a ~130-byte text stub where it expected bytes, and the
# failure is silent in the worst possible way: the Pages deploy ships a "PDF"
# that is a pointer file, and the download 200s with the wrong content.
# ADR-0142 section 2.1 names the two trees a build reads:
#   website-v2/public/  -- vite copies it into dist, and pdflatex
#                          \includegraphics resolves the plates beneath it
#   core/pd-console/    -- test fixtures; a test that needs the network is not a test
#
# Enumerated per extension rather than as `website-v2/public/** !filter ...` on
# purpose: a bare `**` would also unset `diff` and `merge` for the .ts/.json
# files in those trees, silently stripping the very attributes the block at the
# top of this file exists to guarantee. (Verified with `git check-attr`:
# core/pd-console/src/main.rs still reports diff=set merge=set text=set.) The
# list is mechanical; keep it complete rather than trimming it to the
# extensions present today, because a missing line means a pointer served to a
# real user.
website-v2/public/**/*.png   !filter !diff !merge -text
website-v2/public/**/*.jpg   !filter !diff !merge -text
website-v2/public/**/*.jpeg  !filter !diff !merge -text
website-v2/public/**/*.gif   !filter !diff !merge -text
website-v2/public/**/*.webp  !filter !diff !merge -text
website-v2/public/**/*.tiff  !filter !diff !merge -text
website-v2/public/**/*.mp4   !filter !diff !merge -text
website-v2/public/**/*.mov   !filter !diff !merge -text
website-v2/public/**/*.webm  !filter !diff !merge -text
website-v2/public/**/*.pdf   !filter !diff !merge -text
core/pd-console/**/*.png     !filter !diff !merge -text
core/pd-console/**/*.jpg     !filter !diff !merge -text
core/pd-console/**/*.jpeg    !filter !diff !merge -text
core/pd-console/**/*.gif     !filter !diff !merge -text
core/pd-console/**/*.webp    !filter !diff !merge -text
core/pd-console/**/*.tiff    !filter !diff !merge -text
core/pd-console/**/*.mp4     !filter !diff !merge -text
core/pd-console/**/*.mov     !filter !diff !merge -text
core/pd-console/**/*.webm    !filter !diff !merge -text
core/pd-console/**/*.pdf     !filter !diff !merge -text

# CARVE-OUT, and it is not cosmetic.
# tests/unit/spawn-whitepaper-contract.test.js pins the sha256 of
# all-seven-volumes-color-contact-sheet.png and -color-tour.gif, and asserts the
# PNG is exactly 3792x3576 by parsing the IHDR out of the file it reads. Under
# LFS those reads return a ~131-byte pointer: the digests do not match and the
# dimension parse fails. This subtree therefore stays in plain git. Found first
# by branch claude/lfs-evidence-media; re-verified here before being carried over.
#
# NOTE FOR #10199: this subtree sits inside `docs/artifacts/`, an ADR-0142
# OFFLOAD_ROOT, and both pinned files ARE in media/r2-manifest.json. Phase 2
# (deleting offloaded files from git) breaks this same test for the same reason.
docs/artifacts/whitepaper-figure-semantics/** -filter -diff -merge -text
